Good call pushing on. Here's both:

## 1) Resolved row per email

This is hard to do cleanly in a single formula because you need coalesce-across-rows logic per email. The least painful approach uses a helper sheet plus `LAMBDA`/`BYROW`, but honestly a short script is simpler and faster here. I'll give you both so you can pick.

### Formula approach (on a new sheet named `Resolved`)

Assuming cleaned data lives in `Clean!A2:J` with columns: A Timestamp, B Name, C Email, D JoinedBefore, E Phone, F PreferredPark, and G–J are your normalized versions (Yes/No in G, Phone in H, Timestamp in I, Park in J).

**Resolved!A1** (header row, then formula):
```
={"Email","Timestamp","Name","JoinedBefore","Phone","PreferredPark";
  MAP(UNIQUE(FILTER(Clean!C2:C, Clean!C2:C<>"")),
    LAMBDA(em,
      LET(
        rows, FILTER(Clean!A2:J, Clean!C2:C=em),
        sorted, SORT(rows, 9, FALSE),
        latest_ts, INDEX(sorted,1,9),
        name,  IFERROR(INDEX(FILTER(INDEX(sorted,,2), INDEX(sorted,,2)<>""),1),""),
        yn,    IFERROR(INDEX(FILTER(INDEX(sorted,,7), INDEX(sorted,,7)<>"REVIEW", INDEX(sorted,,7)<>""),1),""),
        phone, IFERROR(INDEX(FILTER(INDEX(sorted,,8), INDEX(sorted,,8)<>"REVIEW", INDEX(sorted,,8)<>""),1),""),
        park,  IFERROR(INDEX(FILTER(INDEX(sorted,,10), INDEX(sorted,,10)<>"REVIEW", INDEX(sorted,,10)<>""),1),""),
        {em, latest_ts, name, yn, phone, park}
      )))}
```

How it works: for each unique email, filter all that email's rows, sort by timestamp descending (column 9 of the filtered range = Clean's column I, the parsed timestamp), then for each field take the first non-blank (and non-REVIEW) value from the sorted list. The latest row provides the base; if its field is blank, the next most recent non-blank wins.

**Caveats:**
- Needs `Clean!I` (parsed timestamp) populated for sort to work. Rows with `REVIEW` timestamps will sort as text and may land at the top — filter those out first, or add `FILTER(..., ISNUMBER(INDEX(sorted,,9)))`.
- Slow-ish on 420 rows but fine.
- If you want to include Phone/Park values that are `REVIEW` rather than skip them, drop those `<>"REVIEW"` clauses.

### Script approach (much simpler, recommended)

```javascript
function resolveByEmail() {
  const ss = SpreadsheetApp.getActive();
  const src = ss.getSheetByName('Clean');
  const data = src.getRange(2, 1, src.getLastRow()-1, 10).getValues();
  // Columns: 0 Timestamp, 1 Name, 2 Email, 3 JoinedBefore, 4 Phone,
  //          5 PreferredPark, 6 YN_clean, 7 Phone_clean, 8 TS_clean, 9 Park_clean

  const byEmail = {};
  data.forEach(r => {
    const em = String(r[2]).trim().toLowerCase();
    if (!em) return;
    (byEmail[em] = byEmail[em] || []).push(r);
  });

  const out = [['Email','Timestamp','Name','JoinedBefore','Phone','PreferredPark']];
  const isBad = v => v === '' || v === null || v === 'REVIEW';

  Object.keys(byEmail).forEach(em => {
    const rows = byEmail[em].sort((a,b) => (b[8]||0) - (a[8]||0)); // newest first
    const pick = col => {
      for (const r of rows) if (!isBad(r[col])) return r[col];
      return '';
    };
    out.push([em, rows[0][8], pick(1), pick(6), pick(7), pick(9)]);
  });

  const dest = ss.getSheetByName('Resolved') || ss.insertSheet('Resolved');
  dest.clear();
  dest.getRange(1, 1, out.length, out[0].length).setValues(out);
}
```

Run it once; re-run whenever Clean changes. Easier to debug and much faster than the array formula.

## 2) Conditional formatting rule

Select the full data range (e.g., `A2:P421`), then **Format > Conditional formatting > Custom formula is**:

```
=OR($P2="REVIEW", $G2="REVIEW", $H2="REVIEW", $I2="REVIEW", $J2="REVIEW")
```

Pick a fill color (pale yellow works well). The `$` before the column letters keeps the check tied to those columns while highlighting the whole row.

If you also want duplicates highlighted a different color, add a second rule below it:
```
=OR($M2<>"", $N2<>"")
```
with a pale orange fill.

## Park entries

Yes, paste the 8–12 REVIEW park entries. I'll suggest canonical mappings and flag any that look ambiguous (e.g., "south" alone might not be enough to tell which park). Also helpful: tell me the canonical park names you've settled on so I map to those exact strings.
